</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Listwise Comparative Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The evaluation of listwise ranking, as summarized in Table 3, reveals clear performance differences among scoring paradigms under different context windowing strategies.
Under single-shot listwise inference (window size = 100),</span> <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">internal attention</span> (IA) exhibits clear superiority, achieving an <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nDCG@10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nDCG@10</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.4571 <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">for All-ICR, followed closely by Selective-ICR at 0.4534 with only a marginal reduction in latency. Both</span> substantially exceed the results of both <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generation</span>-based scoring (Gen, 0.4281) and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">likelihood</span>-based scoring (Lik, 0.3650).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the performance rankings shift markedly when adopting a sliding-window strategy (window size = 20, step = 10). While this iterative approach allows Gen and Lik scoring to <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">improve over their single-shot performance</span> from 0.4281 and 0.3650 to 0.4526 and 0.4398 respectively, IA generally struggles with the loss of global context. Specifically, the baseline All-ICR experiences a performance drop from 0.4571 to 0.4354, while its latency climbs to 6.28s due to the overhead of multiple forward passes. <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Importantly, Selective-ICR remains robust under this constrained setting, achieving an <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nDCG@10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nDCG@10</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.4440—ranking second overall and slightly below Gen (0.4526)—while maintaining a lower inference time (4.97s vs. 22.78s). This robustness is particularly relevant in practice, as the effective input context of an LLM is bounded by its architectural context limit, constrained by GPU memory and inference configuration, and further shaped by task-specific prompt and ranking design. As a result, ranking frameworks frequently operate under restricted context windows, even for long-context models.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While Selective-ICR remains competitive, the overall downward trend suggests that IA-based signals fundamentally rely on all-to-all attention over the full candidate set; when restricted to local windows, their global discriminative power diminishes, making them less compatible with iterative refinement strategies that typically favor generative and likelihood-based rankers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Setwise Comparative Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Moving to Setwise frameworks exposes the inherent limitations of internal attention signals when restricted to local contexts. While IA is efficient in single-pass listwise settings, its integration into the sorting logic of heapsort and bubblesort reveals a high fixed per-call cost. <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This overhead persists regardless of the setwise parameter <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and primarily arises from the dual-pass calibration requirement, in which the model must execute two full forward passes per comparison step.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The impact of this calibration overhead is explicitly quantified in our ablation variant. As shown in Table 3, removing the calibration pass (Selective-ICR &amp; w/o Calibration) reduces the heapsort latency from 36.01s to 17.75s. This 50.7% decrease confirms the dual-pass requirement as the primary computational bottleneck. However, even with this optimization, IA remains remarkably slower than generative-based paradigms, with Gen and Lik scoring requiring only 14.11s and 8.86s, respectively. Due to the iterative nature of setwise comparisons, the cumulative computational burden of IA becomes prohibitive. Consequently, this makes IA unsuitable for frameworks designed for fine-grained, repeated updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The bubblesort results further highlight the limitations of internal attention–based scoring under highly localized comparisons. Among all configurations, Lik scoring with setwise bubblesort achieves the highest effectiveness, reaching an average <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nDCG@10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">@</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nDCG@10</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.4999 and slightly outperforming Gen (0.4967). However, this effectiveness comes at a substantial computational cost, with Lik and Gen incurring latencies of 31.54s and 50.94s, respectively. IA-based variants are therefore omitted, as their per-comparison overhead becomes prohibitive under the large number of fine-grained swaps required by bubblesort, yielding impractical runtimes without corresponding gains. Overall, explicit output–based methods remain an effective and feasible choice for highly iterative setwise ranking.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Robustness across Reasoning Domains</h2>

<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
